                <article class="article-body">

                    <!-- Introduction -->
                    <div class="lead-intro">
                        <p>An Overseas Citizen of India (OCI) cardholder may need to update passport details, correct personal information, report a lost card or address a discrepancy between the OCI record and the current passport.</p>
                        <p>For families living overseas, these issues can create uncertainty, particularly when a passport is renewed shortly before travel to India.</p>
                        <p>The Government of India has introduced changes to OCI documentation procedures, including the electronic OCI card (e-OCI) and revised arrangements for updating passport particulars. As a result, older articles describing mandatory physical-card reissuance may no longer reflect the current procedure.</p>
                        <p>The first step is to identify the exact service required. A new passport, a change of personal particulars, a lost OCI card and an incorrect OCI record are different situations and may require different procedures.</p>
                        <p>This guide explains the principal issues OCI cardholders should check before applying for an update or replacement.</p>
                        <p>This article provides general information. Always verify the current instructions on the official OCI portal and with the Indian Mission, Post or FRRO responsible for your case.</p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Section 1 -->
                    <section id="section-1">
                        <h2>1. Does an OCI Card Need to Be Reissued When a New Passport Is Issued?</h2>
                        <p>The Government of India announced changes to OCI documentation procedures in July 2026, including discontinuing the requirement to reissue the physical OCI card upon issuance of a new passport.</p>
                        <p>Under the updated approach, OCI cardholders must update their passport particulars through the prescribed online process. The revised guidance also addresses biometric information requirements.</p>
                        <p>Accordingly, OCI cardholders should not automatically follow older instructions stating that a physical OCI card must be reissued every time a passport is renewed.</p>
                        <p>Before applying, check:</p>
                        <ul class="styled-checklist">
                            <li>Whether the new passport details have been updated in the OCI system.</li>
                            <li>Whether the prescribed passport copy and recent photograph must be uploaded.</li>
                            <li>Whether biometric information must be provided under the applicable procedure.</li>
                            <li>Whether any special requirements apply because of the cardholder's age or registration category.</li>
                            <li>Whether the current OCI record is available electronically through the official portal.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>The relevant instructions should be checked at the time of application because procedures may differ according to the service requested.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 2 -->
                    <section id="section-2">
                        <h2>2. What Is the Procedure After Renewing a Passport?</h2>
                        <p>When an OCI cardholder receives a new foreign passport, the passport particulars should be updated through the prescribed OCI online service.</p>
                        <p>The current instructions should be consulted to establish the applicable deadline, supporting documents and whether any in-person step is required.</p>
                        <p>Depending on the case, relevant documents may include:</p>
                        <ul class="styled-checklist">
                            <li>The current passport containing the holder's photograph.</li>
                            <li>The previous passport, where available and required.</li>
                            <li>The existing OCI registration details.</li>
                            <li>A recent photograph meeting the prescribed specifications.</li>
                            <li>Additional records required for a special category of applicant.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>The process should be completed using the official OCI portal rather than an unofficial website.</p>
                        <div class="callout-box">
                            <div class="callout-title">
                                <i class="bi bi-lightbulb-fill text-gold"></i> Practical Recommendation
                            </div>
                            <p>Do not leave passport-detail updates until immediately before travel. Check the current requirements when the new passport is issued and retain the online acknowledgement or other evidence of the completed update.</p>
                        </div>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 3 -->
                    <section id="section-3">
                        <h2>3. What If the OCI Cardholder Is a Child?</h2>
                        <p>Minor OCI cardholders should follow the current passport-update procedure applicable to their circumstances.</p>
                        <p>Parents should check the child's age, passport details, existing OCI record and the current requirements for photographs and supporting documents.</p>
                        <p>The rules and procedures applicable to children may differ from those for adults, particularly where the identity photograph, parental information or other personal particulars need updating.</p>
                        <p>Parents should verify:</p>
                        <ul class="styled-checklist">
                            <li>That the child's current passport details are correctly recorded.</li>
                            <li>Whether a recent photograph must be uploaded.</li>
                            <li>Whether the existing OCI record matches the child's identity documents.</li>
                            <li>Whether any additional documents are required.</li>
                            <li>Whether the child's details need correction rather than a routine passport update.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Where the child's name or date of birth differs across documents, the discrepancy should be addressed using the procedure applicable to the specific correction.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 4 -->
                    <section id="section-4">
                        <h2>4. What If the OCI Cardholder Is a Foreign Spouse of an Indian Citizen or OCI Cardholder?</h2>
                        <p>Spouse-based OCI registration can involve additional requirements.</p>
                        <p>The current official guidance should be checked for any declarations or supporting documents required when the spouse's passport is renewed.</p>
                        <p>Depending on the applicable procedure, the documents may include the new passport, a recent photograph, a declaration that the marriage continues to subsist and relevant details of the Indian-citizen or OCI-cardholder spouse.</p>
                        <p>The applicant should not assume that the requirements applicable to a person registered through Indian ancestry are identical to those for a person registered on the basis of marriage.</p>
                        <p>Where the marriage has ended, the spouse has remarried or the spouse's status has changed, the matter may require a separate legal assessment rather than a routine passport update.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 5 -->
                    <section id="section-5">
                        <h2>5. What Is an e-OCI Card?</h2>
                        <p>The Government of India has introduced the electronic OCI card, known as e-OCI.</p>
                        <p>The e-OCI is an electronic version of the OCI card that can be accessed through the official OCI system. It contains the particulars held in the OCI records and provides a digital means of accessing the cardholder's information.</p>
                        <p>The official portal states that the e-OCI does not replace the validity or status of existing OCI cardholders. Existing physical OCI booklets continue to be accepted under the published guidance.</p>
                        <p>The e-OCI facility is intended to make access to OCI records more convenient. Cardholders should use the official portal and follow the prescribed process to access or download their electronic card.</p>
                        <p>Before travelling, check the current instructions concerning the documents and electronic records that should be carried or made available.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 6 -->
                    <section id="section-6">
                        <h2>6. What If the OCI Card Is Lost, Stolen or Damaged?</h2>
                        <p>A lost or damaged OCI card is different from a routine passport update.</p>
                        <p>The cardholder should consult the current OCI miscellaneous-services instructions and establish which application, report or supporting evidence is required.</p>
                        <p>Depending on the circumstances, relevant steps may include:</p>
                        <ul class="styled-checklist">
                            <li>Recording the circumstances of the loss or damage.</li>
                            <li>Obtaining a police or other relevant report where required.</li>
                            <li>Locating any available copy of the existing OCI card.</li>
                            <li>Submitting the prescribed online application.</li>
                            <li>Uploading the documents required for the relevant service.</li>
                            <li>Following any verification or interview instructions issued by the competent authority.</li>
                            <li>Retaining the acknowledgement and subsequent correspondence.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>The precise procedure, applicable fee and verification requirements should be checked on the official portal.</p>
                        <p>Do not assume that the process for a lost card is identical to the procedure for correcting personal information or updating a passport.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 7 -->
                    <section id="section-7">
                        <h2>7. What If the Name or Date of Birth Is Incorrect on the OCI Record?</h2>
                        <p>An error in an OCI record should be addressed through the appropriate correction procedure.</p>
                        <p>Examples include:</p>
                        <ul class="styled-checklist">
                            <li>A spelling difference in the cardholder's name.</li>
                            <li>An incorrect date of birth.</li>
                            <li>An error in the father's or mother's name.</li>
                            <li>A discrepancy between the passport and OCI record.</li>
                            <li>A change in nationality or another personal particular.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>The applicant should identify the source of the error and collect supporting evidence before submitting the correction request.</p>
                        <p>Depending on the correction, relevant documents may include the current passport, birth certificate, official name-change record or other evidence accepted by the competent authority.</p>
                        <p>Where several documents contain inconsistent information, the applicant should not assume that changing one record automatically corrects every other record.</p>
                        <p>A structured document review can help determine which records need correction and what evidence should accompany the request.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 8 -->
                    <section id="section-8">
                        <h2>8. Can an OCI Cardholder Update an Address or Occupation?</h2>
                        <p>The OCI miscellaneous-services framework includes procedures for updating certain personal particulars, including address and occupation.</p>
                        <p>The applicable process should be checked on the official portal.</p>
                        <p>Before submitting an update, establish:</p>
                        <ul class="styled-checklist">
                            <li>Which particulars have changed.</li>
                            <li>Whether supporting evidence is required.</li>
                            <li>Whether an online declaration or document upload is necessary.</li>
                            <li>Whether the service is free of charge or attracts a fee under the current instructions.</li>
                            <li>Whether the update has been acknowledged or completed.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Cardholders should retain evidence of the submission and any confirmation received.</p>
                        <p>An address or occupation update should not be confused with a change in nationality or another alteration that may require a different procedure.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 9 -->
                    <section id="section-9">
                        <h2>9. Do OCI Updates Require a Visit to an Indian Mission or FRRO?</h2>
                        <p>Not every OCI service follows the same procedure.</p>
                        <p>Some updates may be completed through the online system, while other matters may require document verification, biometric information or attendance before the competent authority.</p>
                        <p>The applicable process depends on the service requested and the current instructions.</p>
                        <p>Before arranging travel or an appointment, check:</p>
                        <ul class="styled-checklist">
                            <li>Whether the application is entirely online.</li>
                            <li>Whether original documents must be presented.</li>
                            <li>Whether biometric information is required.</li>
                            <li>Whether an appointment is necessary.</li>
                            <li>Which Indian Mission, Post or FRRO has jurisdiction.</li>
                            <li>Whether any additional requirements apply to the applicant's category.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Do not rely on an old checklist or another person's experience without verifying that it applies to the current application.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 10 -->
                    <section id="section-10">
                        <h2>10. What Documents Should Be Prepared?</h2>
                        <p>The precise checklist depends on the service requested.</p>
                        <p>For a routine passport update, the applicant may need the current passport, relevant OCI particulars and a recent photograph.</p>
                        <p>For a lost card, the checklist may include evidence of the loss and a police report where required.</p>
                        <p>For a correction of personal details, the applicant may need documents proving the correct information.</p>
                        <p>For a spouse-based registration, additional declarations or spouse-related records may be required under the applicable procedure.</p>
                        <p>Before submitting the application, compare the proposed document set against the current official instructions.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 11 -->
                    <section id="section-11">
                        <h2>11. Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
                        <p>OCI cardholders should avoid the following errors:</p>
                        <ul class="styled-checklist warning-checklist">
                            <li>Following outdated guidance on physical-card reissuance.</li>
                            <li>Assuming that every new passport requires the same application.</li>
                            <li>Using an unofficial website to submit personal information.</li>
                            <li>Uploading documents with inconsistent names or dates without explanation.</li>
                            <li>Assuming that a passport update automatically corrects other personal particulars.</li>
                            <li>Ignoring special requirements applicable to spouse-based registrations.</li>
                            <li>Assuming that a lost-card application follows the same process as a routine update.</li>
                            <li>Failing to retain application acknowledgements and supporting records.</li>
                            <li>Relying on an estimated processing time without checking the current official guidance.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>The safest approach is to identify the correct service, verify the latest instructions and prepare the required documents before submission.</p>
                    </section>

                    <!-- Section 12 -->
                    <section id="section-12">
                        <h2>12. How RRC Law Associates Can Assist</h2>
                        <p>An OCI update may appear straightforward, but discrepancies between passports, OCI records and supporting documents can create uncertainty.</p>
                        <p>RRC Law Associates can assist with a structured review of matters involving:</p>
                        <ul class="styled-checklist">
                            <li>Passport-detail updates.</li>
                            <li>Incorrect names or personal particulars.</li>
                            <li>Lost or damaged OCI documentation.</li>
                            <li>Supporting-document consistency.</li>
                            <li>OCI records involving children.</li>
                            <li>Spouse-based documentation issues.</li>
                            <li>Historical or ancestry documents relevant to OCI records.</li>
                            <li>Identifying the appropriate official procedure for the matter.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>The scope of assistance depends on the documents and circumstances involved.</p>
                        <p>The competent Indian authority remains responsible for deciding applications and determining whether the prescribed requirements have been satisfied.</p>
                    </section>

